NAME
    awk - filter and reshape text records by field

SYNOPSIS
    awk [-F sep] 'program' [path]

DESCRIPTION
    A focused AWK subset for filtering and reshaping command output or
    files inside the shell sandbox. Reads piped standard input when
    present, otherwise reads [path].

    Supported patterns:
        (empty)         Matches every line.
        /regex/         Matches lines matching a Rust regular expression
                        (no look-around or back-references).
        NR op N         Compares the 1-based record number using ==, !=,
                        <, <=, >, or >=.
        BEGIN, END      Runs once before the first line or after the
                        last line.

    Supported actions:
        {print expr[, expr...][; print ...]}
                        Prints expressions separated by single spaces.
                        Supported expressions are $0, $N (1-based
                        field), $NF, NR, NF, double-quoted strings, and
                        numeric literals. A pattern with no action
                        defaults to {print $0}; an empty {} does
                        nothing.

    By default, fields split on runs of whitespace with leading and
    trailing whitespace trimmed. With -F, fields split on a literal
    separator string (not a regex).

OPTIONS
    -F sep, -Fsep
        Set the literal field separator. Escape sequences \t and \n in
        sep are translated to tab and newline characters.

EXAMPLES
    regs | awk '{print $1}'
    watch log 1 | awk -F, 'NR>1{print $2}'
    cat /Logs/run.log | awk 'NR==2'
    awk -F'\t' '{print $1, $NF}' /Logs/table.tsv

EXIT STATUS
    0   Program ran successfully.
    1   Missing program, syntax error, path outside sandbox, or read
        error.

SEE ALSO
    grep(1), sed(1), sort(1), uniq(1), wc(1)
